ns1:PGO-00000014 a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "protein"@en ;
    dcterms:description "**Round 1** (2024-08-01) The first session reviewed multiple definitions of \"protein\" from various ontological and organisational sources to inform the PGO conceptual alignment. The NCI Thesaurus ID C17021 was initially preferred by three of the funding partners as providing the most broadly applicable general definition. However, the discussion identified several limitations and divergences: 1. **Synthesis route** — a distinction between naturally occurring and synthetically produced proteins. NCIT_C17021 appears to refer to naturally occurring proteins only. 2. **Functional role** — ambiguity over whether proteins should be classified as therapeutic agents (e.g. monoclonal antibodies). 3. **Ontological role** — whether a protein is conceptualised as a substance or as a target entity in biomedical contexts. The IDMP Ontology (IDMP-O) \"protein substance\" definition was noted but set aside: in IDMP-O, \"substance\" is a specification, which limits its utility for PGO's broader semantic scope. Adjacent domains (e.g. production) were flagged as relevant. Organisational definitions were also considered: AstraZeneca provided a classical biochemical definition without a URI (\"A Protein is a nitrogenous organic compound made of large molecules...\"), emphasising structural and enzymatic functions. Roche offered a definition adapted from NCIt, focusing on genetically encoded entities with specific structural and functional properties. The session did not reach final consensus and a follow-up was scheduled. **Outcome:** Further input needed — follow-up session scheduled for 2024-08-15. **Round 2** (2024-08-15) The follow-up session addressed specific issues identified with NCIT_C17021: **Plural semantics**: the definition refers to proteins as a group rather than defining a single instance. **L- vs D-amino acids**: the definition explicitly mentions \"alpha-L-amino acids\", which excludes synthetic D-amino acid-containing peptides. **Natural protein constraint**: the definition is limited to naturally occurring proteins. The Protein Ontology definition (PR:000000001 — \"An amino acid chain that is produced de novo by ribosome-mediated translation of a genetically-encoded mRNA, and any derivatives thereof.\") was discussed as an alternative but also found problematic for its focus on the natural synthetic route. The expert group converged on a preference for definitions that describe the intrinsic, substance-based identity of proteins — rather than definitions constrained by method of synthesis or occurrence. AstraZeneca proposed the Molecular Interactions controlled vocabulary definition: *\"A linear polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds in a specific sequence.\"* (MI_0326 — http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MI_0326). This definition was noted for its generality, broad expert support, and compatibility with IDMP-O. **Outcome:** Consensus reached on MI_0326 as the preferred definition. Formal recommendation submitted to the Steering Group."@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "PGO-00000014" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:rights "HUPO-PSI Molecular Interactions Controlled Vocabulary, released under CC BY 4.0. https://github.com/HUPO-PSI/psi-mi-CV"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MI_0326> ;
    skos:broadMatch <http://hl7.org/fhir/substanceprotein>,
        <https://bioschemas.org/Protein>,
        schema:Protein ;
    skos:broader ns1:PGO-00000000 ;
    skos:definition "A linear polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds in a specific sequence."@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Protein: A Protein is a nitrogenous organic compound made of large molecules composed of one or more long chains of amino acids. Proteins are an essential part of all living organisms, especially as structural components of body tissues such as muscle, hair, etc., and as enzymes and antibodies. [No URI provided]"@en,
        "Protein: A biological macromolecule minimally consisting of one polypeptide chain synthesized at the ribosome [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_36080]"@en,
        "Protein: A group of complex organic macromolecules composed of one or more chains (linear polymers) of alpha-L-amino acids linked by peptide bonds and ranging in size from a few thousand to over 1 million Daltons. Proteins are fundamental genetically encoded components of living cells with specific structures and functions dictated by amino acid sequence. [https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C17021]"@en,
        "Protein: An amino acid chain that is produced de novo by ribosome-mediated translation of a genetically-encoded mRNA, and any derivatives thereof. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000000001]"@en,
        "Protein: An organic molecular entity that is composed of one or more chains (linear polymers) of alpha-L-amino acids linked by peptide bonds, ranging in size from a few thousand to over 1 million Daltons, and is a genetically encoded component of a living cell with specific structures and functions dictated by its amino acid sequence. [Adapted from NCIT — no distinct URI]"@en,
        "ProteinSubstance: A single substance with a defined sequence of alpha-amino acids connected through peptide bonds [https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/idmp/ontology/ISO/ISO11238-Substances/ProteinSubstance]"@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000000001>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8054> ;
    skos:example [ dcterms:description "Cell-adhesion protein; altered in several carcinomas"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.uniprot.org/uniprot/P22223> ;
            rdf:value "Cadherin-3 (CDH3)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "UniProt" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Key tumour suppressor protein; most frequently mutated gene in human cancer"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P04637/entry> ;
            rdf:value "tumor protein p53 (TP53)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "UniProt" ],
        [ dcterms:description "G-protein-coupled receptor; target of antipsychotic drugs"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P14416/entry> ;
            rdf:value "DRD2 (Dopamine receptor D2)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "UniProt" ] ;
    skos:inScheme ns1:PGOConceptScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "protein"@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2025-03-12"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used "2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md",
        "2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md" ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo "pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py" ;
    ns1:preferredSource [ a void:Dataset ;
            dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
            dcterms:publisher "UniProt Consortium (EMBL-EBI / SIB / PIR)" ;
            dcterms:rights "https://www.uniprot.org/help/license" ;
            dcterms:title "UniProt" ;
            dcterms:type "database" ;
            void:uriSpace "https://www.uniprot.org" ;
            skos:note "De facto standard for protein sequence and functional annotation. Research & Early Development" ;
            skos:scopeNote "UniProt accession numbers are stable persistent identifiers for protein instances; covers reviewed (Swiss-Prot) and unreviewed (TrEMBL) entries" ;
            dcat:endpointURL <https://rest.uniprot.org/> ;
            sh:pattern "[A-N,R-Z][0-9][A-Z][A-Z,0-9][A-Z,0-9][0-9] or [O,P,Q][0-9][A-Z,0-9][A-Z,0-9][A-Z,0-9][0-9]" ;
            ns1:identifierResolvability "yes" ;
            ns1:natureOfLicence "open — no conditions" ] .

ns1:PGO-00000015 a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "species"@en ;
    dcterms:description "**Round 1** (2024-08-29) The session reviewed candidate definitions and immediately surfaced three key issues: 1. \"Capable of breeding and producing fertile offspring\" — unnecessarily restrictive; excludes asexual reproducers. 2. \"Living\" — potentially excludes viruses, which are not universally classified as living organisms. 3. **Virus inclusion is a must** — given the importance of viruses in pharma (vaccines, viral vector therapies). The group requested a community email consultation to gather input on which reference definition could include \"virus\" in \"species\". Requests sent to all PGO experts on 2024-08-30. **Outcome:** Email consultation initiated. Follow-up session scheduled. **Round 2** (2024-09-11) The session focused on selecting the most pragmatic definition accommodating viruses. Key findings: The virus/species issue has not been resolved across the field. NCBI taxonomy (the global authority) defines species loosely and excludes virus by definition, although including virus within its subclasses. The group considered adopting NCBI Taxon vocabulary while noting this problem. The original NCBITaxon_species definition link was not found — NCBI does not formally define \"species\". UniProt Taxon definition (*\"An element of a taxonomy for classifying life forms.\"*) was selected as: Short and practical. Does not include the breeding criterion. Does not explicitly exclude virus (interpretation of \"life forms\" can accommodate viruses pragmatically). CC BY 4.0 license. The group recommended that \"virus\" be explicitly acknowledged as considered part of the definition, even if the philosophical question about virus as a \"life form\" remains open. **Outcome:** Consensus on UniProt Taxon definition. Virus inclusion acknowledged pragmatically. Recommendation submitted to Steering Group."@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "PGO-00000015" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:rights "\"UniProt data is made available under a Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY 4.0) License. © UniProt Consortium.\""@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://purl.uniprot.org/html/index-en.html#Taxon> ;
    skos:altLabel "organism"@en ;
    skos:broader ns1:PGO-00000000 ;
    skos:definition "An element of a taxonomy for classifying life forms."@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Species: A group of organisms that differ from all other groups of organisms and that are capable of breeding and producing fertile offspring. [https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C45293]"@en,
        "Species: A species is a population of organisms in which any two individuals of the appropriate sexes or mating types can produce fertile offspring, typically by sexual reproduction. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Species]"@en,
        "Species: A type of taxonomic rank qualifying a living entity. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_species]"@en,
        "Species: Taxonomic group of organisms that differ from all other groups of organisms and that are capable of breeding and producing fertile offspring. [https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/idmp/ontology/ISO/ISO11238-Substances/Species]"@en,
        "Taxon: A set of organisms asserted to represent a natural cohesive biological unit. [https://schema.org/Taxon]"@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Taxon> ;
    skos:example [ dcterms:description "Human species"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://identifiers.org/taxonomy:9606> ;
            rdf:value "Homo sapiens" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "NCBI Taxonomy" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Mouse species (common model organism)"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://identifiers.org/taxonomy:10090> ;
            rdf:value "Mus musculus" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "NCBI Taxonomy" ] ;
    skos:inScheme ns1:PGOConceptScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "species"@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2025-06-12"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used "2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md",
        "2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md" ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo "pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py" ;
    ns1:preferredSource [ a void:Dataset ;
            dcterms:license <https://www.usa.gov/government-works> ;
            dcterms:publisher "NCBI, NIH" ;
            dcterms:rights "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/home/about/policies/" ;
