ns1:PGO-00000029 a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "molecular target"@en ;
    dcterms:description "**Round 1 *(Phase 1 session)*** (2025-04-10) The alignment meeting evaluated proposed, existing ontology and controlled vocabulary definitions for \"target\" in pharma research and early development (R&ED). The group emphasised that \"molecular target\" is a more appropriate and precise term than the broader \"target\", particularly in R&ED contexts. Multiple sources were reviewed: NCIT C16128 (NCI Thesaurus) — preferred despite lacking a formal definition and framing being limited to malignancies; BAO_0003064 — too specific to assay design; GO:0003674 (Gene Ontology) — strong definition of molecular function but limited to gene products, excludes DNA; DTO_00000004 (Drug Target Ontology) — appropriate for drug development but overly narrow for exploratory research. The group noted that molecular target appears to function more as a \"role\" than as a material entity — consistent with other PGO concepts such as Drug and Active Ingredient, which also carry role-based semantics. **Outcome:** Recommendation to SG: (1) Replace \"target\" with \"molecular_target\" as the label; (2) Provisionally adopt NCIT C16128; (3) Consider minting a new, role-aware definition in the future. **Round 2 *(Phase 2 session)*** (2025-09-04) The group reviewed the pool of candidate definitions following the SG's rejection of NCIT C16128 (Phase 1). Two viable candidates emerged: **Option 1 (preferred):** DTO_00000004 — \"A material entity, such as native (gene product) protein, protein complex, microorganism, DNA, etc., that physically interacts with a therapeutic or prophylactic drug (with some binding affinity) and where this physical interaction is (at least partially) the cause of a (detectable) clinical effect.\" Favoured by (Darko Hric), Ammar Ammar, an expert, and for its good coverage of entity types and explicit causal mechanism. Giovanni flagged the label mismatch (Drug_Target vs. Molecular_Target). **Option 2 (alternative):** NCIT C16128 — retained as a fallback but considered less suitable given SG's prior rejection and oncology framing. Open issues raised but not fully resolved: (1) the DTO definition covers drug-interacting targets but not vaccine development contexts (e.g. antigen); (2) pre-pathological cell targets not clearly covered; (3) DTO maintenance concern (not updated since 2018, not in OLS). **Outcome:** Recommendation to SG: adopt DTO_00000004 as working definition, acknowledging limitations; request label alignment to \"Molecular_Target\". Open issues to be noted as pending future definition refinement."@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "PGO-00000029" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:rights "Drug Target Ontology (DTO). Concept DTO_00000004. http://www.drugtargetontology.org/dto/DTO_00000004. Licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/)"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/DTO?p=classes&conceptid=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.drugtargetontology.org%2Fdto%2FDTO_00000004> ;
    skos:altLabel "Biological target"@en,
        "Drug target"@en,
        "Pharmacological target"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <http://hl7.org/fhir/goal>,
        <https://biolink.github.io/biolink-model/target_for>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1047579> ;
    skos:broader ns1:PGO-00000000 ;
    skos:definition "A material entity, such as native (gene product) protein, protein complex, microorganism, DNA, etc., that physically interacts with a therapeutic or prophylactic drug (with some binding affinity) and where this physical interaction is (at least partially) the cause of a (detectable) clinical effect."@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Biological Target: A biological target is a biopolymer such as a protein or nucleic acid whose activity can be modified by an external stimulus. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/CANCO?p=classes&conceptid=http%3A%2F%2Fchem.deri.ie%2Fgranatum%23BiologicalTarget]"@en,
        "Synonym 'Biological target': partial overlap"@en,
        "Synonym 'Drug target': DTO label — broader overlap"@en,
        "Target: A Material Entity that is the object of an Act of Targeting. Although people speak of targeting a process, what are in fact targeted are the material participants. Only material things can be targeted for action. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/SLSO?p=classes&conceptid=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.commoncoreontologies.org%2Font00000544]"@en,
        "protein target: A peptide, protein, or protein complex that is recognized by an antibody. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/ERO?p=classes&conceptid=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FERO_0001545]"@en,
        "target: The role of a molecular entity (protein or nucleic acid) that is the presumed subject of an assay and whose activity is being regulated by an entity with a perturbagen role in that assay. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/BAO?p=classes&conceptid=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.bioassayontology.org%2Fbao%23BAO_0003064]"@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://www.drugtargetontology.org/dto/DTO_00000004> ;
    skos:example [ dcterms:description "MicroRNA target of miravirsen (anti-miR-122 LNA antisense oligonucleotide for HCV treatment); first ncRNA drug target to reach Phase II clinical trials; illustrates the RNA/ncRNA molecular target class"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://rnacentral.org/rna/URS00003380CC/9606> ;
            rdf:value "miR-122-5p (human)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "RNAcentral" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Protein kinase — target of erlotinib and gefitinib in NSCLC"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chembl/explore/target/CHEMBL203> ;
            rdf:value "EGFR (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "ChEMBL" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Oncogenic BCR-ABL p210 fusion protein; target of imatinib in CML"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chembl/explore/target/CHEMBL6105> ;
            rdf:value "BCR-ABL1 fusion kinase" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "ChEMBL" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Receptor tyrosine kinase — target of trastuzumab in HER2+ breast cancer"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chembl/explore/target/CHEMBL1824> ;
            rdf:value "HER2 (ERBB2)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "ChEMBL" ] ;
    skos:inScheme ns1:PGOConceptScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "molecular target"@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2025-09-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used "2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md",
        "2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md" ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo "pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py" ;
    ns1:preferredSource [ a void:Dataset ;
            dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/> ;
            dcterms:publisher "EMBL-EBI" ;
