ns1:PGO-00000030 a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "study subject"@en ;
    dcterms:description "**Round 1 *(Phase 1 session)*** (2025-03-12) The Phase 1 alignment evaluated definitions and labels for the concept \"Subject-Person\". The group concluded that \"Study Subject\" must be sufficiently general to encompass: human participants (clinical trials), animal models, environmental systems (e.g. soil, water, air), microbiome-related samples, and cell cultures. Label \"Subject-Person\" was rejected as too narrow; \"Study_Subject\" accepted as the replacement. NCIT_C41189 (\"A matter or an individual that is observed, analyzed, examined, investigated, experimented upon, or/and treated in the course of a particular study\") was recommended. The group also proposed introducing Clinical_Study_Subject as a separate concept for clinical-domain specificity, adopting NCIT C70668. Data provenance considerations: identifiers for Study_Subject should capture associations with study protocols, samples, datasets, and dates. **Outcome:** Phase 1 recommendation: (1) Label \"Study_Subject\"; (2) NCIT_C41189; (3) Add Clinical_Study_Subject as separate concept. SG rejected at 2025-06-12 — definition must cover non-human pre-clinical subjects. **Round 2** (2025-08-21) The group tested candidate definitions against concrete examples of study subjects: a dog in a veterinary interventional study; a person in a clinical trial; a rat in a preclinical safety study; an aliquot of compound X in a stability study; a dataset in a study testing analytical workflow performance. Context was emphasised: \"Clinical Research Study\" narrows to Clinical Study or Clinical Trial. The group discussed whether \"subject\" should shift to \"participant\" in clinical contexts (recognising patient agency) but concluded that \"Study_Subject\" is the more precise ontological term. The broader question of interoperability across pharma functions and knowledge domains was noted. **Outcome:** No final decision. New candidate definitions gathered. Session continued at 2025-09-18. **Round 3** (2025-09-18) The group converged on the HL7 C1550501 definition (\"The principle target on which the action happens\") as the recommended definition. Philippe Rocca-Serra noted its alignment with an HL7 FHIR resource and that \"action\" covers both observation and intervention, applicable to any study from physical/chemical characterisation to clinical trials. Birgit Meldal: covers everything. An expert: \"action\" is too broad and unclear, but accepted the group consensus. The definition's extensibility was seen as a strength: examples include a patient in physical examination, a specimen in a lab observation, a device as subject of maintenance — coverage extends to environmental systems, microbiome, cell cultures relevant to PGO's pre-clinical scope. The group also confirmed: Clinical_Study_Subject (another concept) is to be modelled as a distinct subclass of Study_Subject. **Outcome:** Recommendation to SG: adopt HL7 C1550501 for Study_Subject. Clinical_Study_Subject established as a separate Phase 2 concept."@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "PGO-00000030" ;
    dcterms:license <http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dcterms:rights "HL7 International. Concept C1550501 (subject). http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/HL7/C1550501"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/HL7/C1550501> ;
    skos:altLabel "Research subject"@en,
        "Study participant"@en,
        "Test subject"@en,
        "Trial subject"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <http://hl7.org/fhir/ResearchSubject>,
        <https://bioschemas.org/BioChemEntity> ;
    skos:broader ns1:PGO-00000000 ;
    skos:definition "The principle target on which the action happens. Examples: The patient in physical examination, a specimen in a lab observation. May also be a patient's family member (teaching) or a device or room (cleaning, disinfecting, housekeeping)."@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Study-subject: A study subject is an individual that is the subject of the study. [http://semanticscience.org/resource/SIO_000399]"@en,
        "Subject: An entity of interest, either biological or otherwise. EXAMPLE(S): A human being who might be of interest because they are on a study, a sheep who might have experienced an adverse event, or a pacemaker that failed. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SLSO_0000034]"@en,
        "Subject: An entity, either biologic or otherwise, of interest in an investigation. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C79916]"@en,
        "Synonym 'Study participant': human-centred contexts; note: \"participant\" implies agency; ontologically \"subject\" is more precise"@en,
        "Synonym 'Trial subject': clinical context — use Clinical_Study_Subject"@en,
        "subject: A living individual about whom a research investigator obtains data through 1) intervention or interaction with the individual, or 2) identifiable private information. [https://hadatac.org/ont/chear/#Subject]"@en,
        "subject: The universal for which we are capturing data for or about. It defines the entity for which we want to relate captured content to (e.g. a car, a person, an animal). Note: a subject is not disjoint with material entity or immaterial entity. [http://purl.org/dco/v1#subject]"@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <https://biolink.github.io/biolink-model/Human>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4204239> ;
    skos:example [ dcterms:description "Human patient study subject in the MSK IMPACT targeted sequencing study"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.cbioportal.org/patient?studyId=msk_impact_2017&caseId=P-0003456> ;
            rdf:value "Patient P-0003456 (MSK IMPACT 2017)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "cBioPortal" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Human patient study subject in The Cancer Genome Atlas glioblastoma cohort"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.cbioportal.org/patient?studyId=gbm_tcga_pub&caseId=TCGA-02-0001> ;
            rdf:value "Patient TCGA-02-0001 (TCGA GBM)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "cBioPortal" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Clinical study subject in the MSK-IMPACT 2017 cohort study"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.cbioportal.org/patient?studyId=msk_impact_2017&caseId=P-0000435> ;
            rdf:value "Patient P-0000435 (MSK IMPACT study)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "cBioPortal" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Human cell-free plasma RNA sample (GEO GSM9866234, normal control) carrying donor id 4335; a non-patient study-subject instance where the sample identifies the subject; part of NCBI BioProject 1492360 (289-sample plasma miRNA study)"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSM9866234> ;
            rdf:value "Plasma RNA sample FA80805Z-06 (donor id 4335)" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "NCBI GEO" ],
        [ dcterms:description "Non-human study-subject instance: a chalk-hill blue butterfly (Lysandra coridon) specimen, specimen_id SAN20004396, where the subject is the sample itself"@en ;
            dcterms:identifier <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/biosamples/samples/SAMEA119944481> ;
            rdf:value "Lysandra coridon specimen SAN20004396" ;
            prov:wasDerivedFrom "EBI BioSamples" ] ;
    skos:inScheme ns1:PGOConceptScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "study subject"@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2025-09-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used "2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md",
        "2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md" ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo "pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py" ;
    ns1:preferredSource [ a void:Dataset ;
            dcterms:license <https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/home/about/policies/> ;
            dcterms:publisher "NCBI (US National Library of Medicine)" ;
            dcterms:rights "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/home/about/policies/" ;
            dcterms:title "NCBI BioSample / BioProject" ;
            dcterms:type "database" ;
            void:uriSpace "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/biosample/" ;
            skos:note "NCBI BioSample / BioProject. Research & Early Development. A sample record can serve as a study-subject instance where it carries a donor ID identifying the subject. NCBI GEO Sample (GSM) records with donor IDs also qualify (e.g. GSM9866234, part of BioProject 1492360)" ;
            skos:scopeNote "Non-patient and sample-linked study-subject instances" ;
            dcat:endpointURL <https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/biosample/> ;
            sh:pattern "SAMN[0-9]+" ;
            ns1:identifierResolvability "yes" ;
            ns1:natureOfLicence "open — no conditions" ],
        [ a void:Dataset ;
            dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
